/**
 * NetGravity — World administrative subdivisions (Natural Earth 10m)
 * ==================================================================
 * States, provinces, prefectures, departments — 4596 of them across 253
 * countries. The political layer under both maps.
 *
 * Source: Natural Earth 1:10m Admin 1 – States, Provinces, via
 * github.com/nvkelso/natural-earth-vector. PUBLIC DOMAIN: no attribution
 * obligation, no API key, no quota. Same source and same terms as
 * `world-basemap.js`, which carries the country outlines these sit inside.
 *
 * WHY IT EXISTS
 * -------------
 * The country layer alone answers "which country is this network in". It
 * cannot answer "which state is this facility in" — and on a US or Indian
 * network that is the question a reader actually has when they look at a map,
 * because that is the level their operations are organised at. With coastline
 * and national borders only, twelve US facilities sat on one undifferentiated
 * shape.
 *
 * LOADED ON DEMAND
 * ----------------
 * 1.6 MB is too much to put in front of a sign-in screen, so this module is
 * NOT imported statically. `map.js` and `twin3d.js` reach it through
 * `loadAdmin1()` in `world-basemap.js`, which dynamic-imports it the first
 * time a map is drawn and caches it. A map renders immediately with countries
 * and gains its internal borders a moment later, rather than the whole
 * application waiting on it.
 *
 * PRECISION
 * ---------
 * Douglas-Peucker at 0.09 degrees, then coordinates rounded to 2 decimal
 * places (~1.1 km). A subdivision narrower than that grid — Vatican City,
 * six Maldivian atolls, three Maltese towns — is rounded to 4 places instead,
 * because a shape smaller than the grid must get a finer grid rather than be
 * rounded out of existence. Outlying islets under 0.12 square degrees are
 * dropped; the body of every subdivision is always kept. Together these take
 * the file from 38.8 MB to 1.6 MB with nothing missing.
 *
 * Geometry is [longitude, latitude] in WGS84 — GeoJSON order, the reverse of
 * Leaflet's own [lat, lng].
 */

